# Basic idea # {#idea}

We are revisiting James Touton's P1219R2 proposal for homogeneous function parameter packs. <sup>[[P1219R2]]</sup>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}
</xmp>

The starting model has one trailing pack. The function is implicitly a template specialized by pack length, even when the element type is fixed. Calls deduce the length and use ordinary parameter initialization, including for runtime arguments.

# Motivation # {#motivation}

Passing element values to a <code>std::initializer_list</code> parameter requires braces and gives the function const access to those elements. Requiring exact types in a deduced pack can reject otherwise valid implicit conversions. <sup>[[P1219R2]]</sup>

Pack constructors would not receive initializer-list constructors' special overload preference. <sup>[[P1219R2]]</sup>

# TODO # {#todo}

- Add a concrete interface example and compare a constrained pack, an initializer list, and a range interface.
- Compare implicit packs with typed runtime arguments, bounded overload families, and checked C varargs.
- Choose new syntax that preserves existing declarations and their meaning, including named and unnamed C varargs. Avoid retiring existing language features.
- Start with named, type-first packs. Compare unnamed-pack markers and declaration contexts. Keep prefix ellipsis and explicit length as alternatives.
- Define pack-length identity separately from deduction. Cover explicit lengths, typed function pointers versus <code>auto</code>, explicit instantiation, and explicit specialization.
- Settle deduction for dependent <code>T</code>, including mixed types and value categories, empty packs, and minimum counts.
- Distinguish maximum counts, checked count arguments, and bounded traversal. Count checks alone do not establish argument types.
- Decide whether the initial scope includes references, lambdas, member functions, and constructors.
- Define reference binding and temporary lifetimes, including move-only arguments. Check braced arguments and overloaded function names.
- Check overload resolution, constructor list-initialization, constraints, <code>noexcept</code>, and constant evaluation.
- Check that separate declarations and definitions agree across translation units and modules.
- Record which P1219R2 rules are retained or changed and why. Discuss the direction with Touton and implementers before drafting standard wording. <sup>[[P1219R2]]</sup>
- Write expected outcomes and rejection cases, then test a prototype. Measure build time and code size across pack lengths and convertible input types.

# Design references # {#design-references}

- P1219R2 discusses template markers and explicit pack lengths alongside its implicit-length model. <sup>[[P1219R2]]</sup>
- The current function declaration rules distinguish packs from C varargs. Comma-less varargs remain permitted but deprecated. Return type deduction is independent of that distinction. <sup>[[CXX-FUNCTIONS]] [[CXX-VARARGS-DEPRECATION]] [[CXX-AUTO]]</sup>
- Existing ellipsis arguments undergo default promotions. This matters when comparing typed packs with checked C varargs. <sup>[[CXX-CALLS]]</sup>
- D's typesafe variadic functions provide a reference for collecting arguments into a typed runtime array. GCC's <code>__builtin_va_arg_pack_len</code> provides a reference for counting trailing arguments, limited to functions guaranteed to be inlined. <sup>[[D-VARIADIC]] [[GCC-CALLS]]</sup>
- Alternative spellings need to account for pack indexing, optional attribute semantics, and existing template-id uses in specialization and friend declarations. <sup>[[CXX-PACK-INDEX]] [[CXX-ATTRIBUTES]] [[CXX-SPECIALIZATION]] [[CXX-TEMPLATE-FRIENDS]]</sup>

